WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.670
<i> NARRATOR: It's the greatest
 aviation mystery of all time.</i>

00:00:04.703 --> 00:00:09.275 align:start position:0%
PILOT: Malaysia 370 contact
Ho Chi Minh on 120.9 Good night.

00:00:09.308 --> 00:00:15.314
<i> NARRATOR: How can a Boeing
 777 with 239 people on board</i>

00:00:15.347 --> 00:00:19.452
<i> simply vanish without a trace?</i>

00:00:22.521 --> 00:00:27.327
<i> The answer lies somewhere at
 the bottom of the Indian Ocean.</i>

00:00:29.728 --> 00:00:36.536
<i> Imagine if we could empty the
 oceans, letting the water drain
 away to reveal the secrets</i>

00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:38.771
<i> of the sea floor.</i>

00:00:38.804 --> 00:00:42.308
<i> Now we can.</i>

00:00:44.543 --> 00:00:49.248 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Using the latest underwater
 scanning technology,
 piercing the deep oceans,</i>

00:00:49.281 --> 00:00:55.188
<i> and turning accurate
 data into 3D images.</i>

00:00:55.221 --> 00:01:00.126
<i> This time, with unique access
 to the official investigation,</i>

00:01:00.159 --> 00:01:03.263 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> key mysteries
 of Malaysia Airlines 370.</i>

00:01:04.997 --> 00:01:09.369 align:start position:0%
<i> What lessons lie amid the wrecs
 of previous air disasters?</i>

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:16.076
<i> Can Cold War technology extract
 vital clues from the deep ocea?</i>

00:01:21.947 --> 00:01:27.420 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> And if the plane is found,
 what secrets could lie hidden
 in the tangled wreckage?</i>

00:02:01.353 --> 00:02:07.994 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> March the 8th 2014,
 Malaysia Airlines flight
 370 departs Kuala Lumpur,</i>

00:02:08.027 --> 00:02:10.964
<i> on a six-hour flight to Beijin.</i>

00:02:19.638 --> 00:02:24.510
<i> NARRATOR: As the Boeing 777
 enters Vietnamese airspace</i>

00:02:24.543 --> 00:02:28.047 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> it suddenly drops off air
 traffic control radar.</i>

00:02:35.354 --> 00:02:41.361 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> A modern commercial
 airliner, carrying 239
 people, has just vanished.</i>

00:02:45.664 --> 00:02:51.371 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> There's no mayday call and
 no wreckage at the point
 of last radar contact.</i>

00:03:01.513 --> 00:03:05.952 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Aviation authorities
 struggle to understand.</i>

00:03:05.985 --> 00:03:09.522
REPRESENTATIVE: We believe
family members should prepare
themselves for the worst.

00:03:09.555 --> 00:03:13.226
(crying)

00:03:13.259 --> 00:03:16.629
<i> NARRATOR: Families left
 behind demand answers.</i>

00:03:16.662 --> 00:03:21.367
WOMAN: (Speaking in Mandarin)

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:25.037 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> NARRATOR: The Malaysian
 government launches an
 investigation.</i>

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:29.142
<i> It will be the largest and most
 expensive in aviation history.</i>

00:03:31.076 --> 00:03:37.049
<i> With multiple dead ends, false
 leads, a deluge of speculation,</i>

00:03:37.082 --> 00:03:39.719 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> but only a handful of real
 clues.</i>

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:44.424 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> One of the first comes from
 the Malaysian military.</i>

00:03:47.426 --> 00:03:53.566 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Powerful defence radar has
 detected the plane, turning
 back across Malaysia,</i>

00:03:53.599 --> 00:03:59.805
<i> heading northwest up the Strait
 of Malacca, then disappearing
 out of range here,</i>

00:03:59.838 --> 00:04:02.041
<i> just north of Sumatra.</i>

00:04:03.208 --> 00:04:05.978
<i> It's a shocking discovery.</i>

00:04:06.011 --> 00:04:10.283 align:start position:0%
<i> After it disappeared from
 civilian radar, it didn't cras.</i>

00:04:10.316 --> 00:04:13.753
<i> It continued flying.</i>

00:04:13.786 --> 00:04:19.392 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
JOHN: Initially when the
airplane made a turn without
talking to air traffic control,

00:04:19.425 --> 00:04:22.428 line:0%
in my mind, all bets were off.

00:04:22.461 --> 00:04:27.133 align:start size:97% position:3%
It could be a terrorist event.
It could be a deliberate act
by a crew member,

00:04:27.166 --> 00:04:30.503
it could be a mass failure
in the electrical system.

00:04:35.074 --> 00:04:39.812 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The next
 clue comes from space.</i>

00:04:39.845 --> 00:04:46.419 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> Although lost to radar, MH 370
 continued to exchange what are
 called 'heartbeat' signals</i>

00:04:46.452 --> 00:04:50.323
<i> with an Inmarsat satellite
 above the Indian Ocean.</i>

00:04:51.757 --> 00:04:55.761 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> The heartbeats
 come once an hour.</i>

00:04:55.794 --> 00:05:01.967 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> Frequency changes in the
 signals help experts calculate
 its direction of travel</i>

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:05.638 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> and reveal something
 extraordinary.</i>

00:05:05.671 --> 00:05:15.648
<i> After its initial track to the
 northwest, MH370 turned south
 and flew for six more hours.</i>

00:05:15.681 --> 00:05:20.319
<i> From the travel time of each
 signal investigators also
 calculate the plane's distance</i>

00:05:20.352 --> 00:05:22.188
<i> from the satellite.</i>

00:05:22.221 --> 00:05:25.758
<i> These lines are called arcs.</i>

00:05:25.791 --> 00:05:31.597 align:start position:0%
<i> Using the fuel load and cruisig
 speed, they can recreate a rane
 of possible flight paths</i>

00:05:31.630 --> 00:05:34.167
<i> as the plane crosses each arc.</i>

00:05:35.634 --> 00:05:40.673
<i> The final, or seventh, arc
 is deep in the Indian Ocean.</i>

00:05:40.706 --> 00:05:44.777
<i> There are no more heartbeat
 signals beyond this point.</i>

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:48.814 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
PETER: The data was
extraordinary in as much as
you're trying to establish

00:05:48.847 --> 00:05:54.287
an aircraft's position based
on information that was never
intended for that purpose.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:58.557 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: It's the biggest
 breakthrough so far</i>

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:07.066 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> instantly shifting the search
 3,000 miles south of the last
 military radar contact</i>

00:06:07.099 --> 00:06:11.337
<i> taking it into the jurisdiction
 of the Australian government,</i>

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:14.573
<i> and accident investigator Peter
 Foley.</i>

00:06:14.606 --> 00:06:19.678 align:start size:97% position:3%
PETER: This is something
extraordinary that was captured
by Inmarsat at the time

00:06:19.711 --> 00:06:26.886 align:start position:0%
the aircraft was in the air,
which very few people knew about
and which is absolutely key

00:06:26.919 --> 00:06:30.757 align:start position:0%
to the search and working out
where to look for that aircraft.

00:06:32.791 --> 00:06:35.494 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> NARRATOR: They
 start looking here:</i>

00:06:35.527 --> 00:06:41.400 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> a swathe of ocean running
 seven hundred miles along
 the seventh arc.</i>

00:06:41.433 --> 00:06:46.272 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> They estimate that the
 plane could lie 140 miles
 either side,</i>

00:06:46.305 --> 00:06:50.042
<i> the maximum glide
 range of a 777.</i>

00:06:55.881 --> 00:07:02.388 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> The seventh arc is over
 1,500 miles from the
 nearest land.</i>

00:07:02.421 --> 00:07:06.426
<i> It will take two days
 for Australian search
 vessels to get there.</i>

00:07:07.559 --> 00:07:12.565
<i> Two powerful storms have swept
 through since MH 370 disappeard</i>

00:07:12.598 --> 00:07:15.902 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> making the job of spotting
 debris even harder.</i>

00:07:17.302 --> 00:07:24.744
<i> 22 aircraft and 19 surface
 vessels look for wreckage,
 and possibly even survivors.</i>

00:07:25.644 --> 00:07:31.050 align:start size:94% position:6%
PETER: When an aircraft
enters the ocean with energy,
you expect to see a potential
oil slick;

00:07:31.083 --> 00:07:35.588 align:start size:91% position:9%
you expect to see all
sorts of objects, actually,
which are buoyant,

00:07:35.621 --> 00:07:39.391
which are released from
the aircraft as debris.

00:07:39.424 --> 00:07:44.196
<i> NARRATOR: Surface debris is
 normally the first step to
 finding a missing aircraft,</i>

00:07:44.229 --> 00:07:46.799
<i> but it's not a guarantee.</i>

00:07:46.832 --> 00:07:52.471
<i> Before MH370, the largest
 and most expensive search in
 aviation history takes place</i>

00:07:52.504 --> 00:07:59.512 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> here, 8,000 miles away in
 the Atlantic Ocean</i>

00:07:59.545 --> 00:08:04.884
<i> hunting for the wreck of
 Air France flight 447.</i>

00:08:04.917 --> 00:08:10.690 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> What lessons lie amid
 this tangled wreckage, for
 those searching for MH370?</i>

00:08:17.796 --> 00:08:23.869
<i> On June the 1ST, 2009,
 Air France 447 leaves
 Rio de Janeiro, Brazil,</i>

00:08:23.902 --> 00:08:31.911
<i> carrying 216 passengers
 and 12 crew to Paris.</i>

00:08:31.944 --> 00:08:36.482 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Weather reports indicate
 severe thunderstorms
 along the flight path.</i>

00:08:38.083 --> 00:08:42.988
<i> Three hours and 45 minutes
 into the flight, as the
 plane nears the equator,</i>

00:08:43.021 --> 00:08:47.526
<i> its on-board computers send a
 burst of emergency warnings</i>

00:08:47.559 --> 00:08:49.996
<i> then falls silent.</i>

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:59.805 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Olivier Ferrante is in charge
 of the French search team.</i>

00:09:00.806 --> 00:09:03.976
<i> The warning signals
 are his first clue.</i>

00:09:04.009 --> 00:09:07.346 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
OLIVIER: Some maintenance
messages, and an important
position message,

00:09:07.379 --> 00:09:11.951 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
which was very useful
for our search.

00:09:11.984 --> 00:09:18.691 align:start position:0%
<i> NARRATOR: It is a deep and
 remote stretch of ocean, but te
 search team has every advantag.</i>

00:09:18.724 --> 00:09:21.293
<i> A known flight path.</i>

00:09:21.326 --> 00:09:23.529
<i> An accurate final position.</i>

00:09:23.562 --> 00:09:28.267
<i> And then, surface debris
 found within five days.</i>

00:09:30.235 --> 00:09:37.509
<i> And yet, despite initial
 optimism, it takes two
 years, four expeditions,</i>

00:09:37.542 --> 00:09:41.313 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> and forty million dollars
 to find the plane.</i>

00:09:41.346 --> 00:09:45.684 align:start size:84% position:16%
OLIVIER: Yes I remember
that moment when
we found the wreckage.

00:09:45.717 --> 00:09:52.458
I was very careful, because
we've had false alerts before
and then it turned out to be

00:09:52.491 --> 00:09:57.796
geology or not what
we were looking for.

00:09:57.829 --> 00:10:01.200
<i> NARRATOR: Why does
 it take so long?</i>

00:10:01.233 --> 00:10:06.005 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Using the data meticulously
 collected by the search team,
 combined with the latest</i>

00:10:06.038 --> 00:10:12.177 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> visualisation technology,
 It is possible to
 drain the Atlantic Ocean</i>

00:10:12.210 --> 00:10:17.850 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> revealing the hostile
 world that faces every
 deep-water search effort.</i>

00:10:18.950 --> 00:10:21.120
<i> Immense water pressure.</i>

00:10:21.153 --> 00:10:26.792
<i> Freezing cold, total darkness,
 and hidden dangers everywhere.</i>

00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:31.830
<i> Finally, at 12,800 feet</i>

00:10:31.863 --> 00:10:36.035 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> the shattered remains
 of Air France 447.</i>

00:10:37.903 --> 00:10:45.678
<i> Engines, wheel assemblies, and
 other heavy items lie together</i>

00:10:45.711 --> 00:10:50.883 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> their twisted remains
 a testament to the
 force of the impact.</i>

00:10:53.251 --> 00:10:58.357 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Smaller, lighter, objects
 stretch along more than a
 third of a mile of sea floor,</i>

00:10:58.390 --> 00:11:02.594 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> equivalent to ten
 New York city blocks.</i>

00:11:02.627 --> 00:11:10.135
<i> And a critical realization:
 the seabed wreckage is nowhere
 near the surface wreckage.</i>

00:11:10.168 --> 00:11:13.806
<i> It's almost 24 miles away.</i>

00:11:13.839 --> 00:11:20.045 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Investigators have wasted
 years searching directly
 underneath the surface debris</i>

00:11:20.078 --> 00:11:28.487 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> not realizing that ocean
 currents have carried it far
 away from the crash site.</i>

00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:33.759 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> The long and frustrating
 search for the French
 aircraft carries a warning.</i>

00:11:33.792 --> 00:11:40.432 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Even with debris and an
 accurate final position, when
 a plane goes into deep water,</i>

00:11:40.465 --> 00:11:42.435
<i> it's very hard to find.</i>

00:11:45.203 --> 00:11:50.109
<i> A sobering thought for the
 team searching for MH370.</i>

00:11:50.142 --> 00:11:56.615 align:start size:94% position:6%
OLIVIER: Air France was very
difficult with our area of
17,000 square kilometres.

00:11:56.648 --> 00:12:05.457 align:start size:97% position:3%
But it's nothing compared
to the the surface that the
search teams have to for MH370.

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:07.760
It's much more difficult.

00:12:10.629 --> 00:12:16.702 align:start position:0%
<i> NARRATOR: In the southern
 Indian Ocean, investigators lok
 for surface debris for over two
 weeks.</i>

00:12:17.702 --> 00:12:22.574 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> Even with the aid of
 satellites, they find nothing.</i>

00:12:22.607 --> 00:12:28.547 align:start size:97% position:3%
JOHN: One of the greatest
initial mysteries and that has
transcended the entire

00:12:28.580 --> 00:12:33.652 align:start size:97% position:3%
period of time that we have
been looking for this
airplane, where is the debris?

00:12:37.322 --> 00:12:41.627
<i> NARRATOR: With no debris
 they need to find another
 way to narrow the search.</i>

00:12:44.796 --> 00:12:50.602 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> So they focus on MH370's
 'black box' data recorders.</i>

00:12:50.635 --> 00:12:55.440
<i> Each has an underwater
 locater beacon or ULB.</i>

00:12:55.473 --> 00:13:02.281 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> In water they emit an
 electronic 'ping' with a
 range of just over a mile.</i>

00:13:02.314 --> 00:13:05.718
<i> Find the beacons and
 you find the plane.</i>

00:13:06.585 --> 00:13:09.254
<i> But time is running out.</i>

00:13:09.287 --> 00:13:12.891 align:start size:91% position:9%
PETER: Battery life on the
ULB's is only 30 days.

00:13:12.924 --> 00:13:16.695
Although they do last
longer they fade out.

00:13:16.728 --> 00:13:20.299 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: By the time
 specialist pinger locater
 technology reaches the search</i>

00:13:20.332 --> 00:13:24.837
<i> zone, there are just three
 days of battery life left.</i>

00:13:29.474 --> 00:13:35.247 line:0%
<i> (music)</i>

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:37.916
O'DELL: Steer 319.
OFFICER: Steer 319.

00:13:37.949 --> 00:13:43.288 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: Investigators have
 only a few days to locate MH
 370's black boxes,</i>

00:13:43.321 --> 00:13:46.792
<i> before their locater
 batteries run out.</i>

00:13:46.825 --> 00:13:52.497 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Faced with a vast search
 zone, they decide to focus
 on commonly used air routes.</i>

00:13:52.530 --> 00:13:58.937 align:start size:97% position:3%
PETER: It was thought that if
the aircraft had been in
distress and was trying to make

00:13:58.970 --> 00:14:02.675 line:0%
to complete a flight for
example to Perth, there was
some recognized air routes.

00:14:05.510 --> 00:14:10.015 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: Australian navy
 vessel Ocean Shield steams to
 the point where the air route</i>

00:14:10.048 --> 00:14:13.185 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> to Perth intersects
 the seventh arc.</i>

00:14:21.192 --> 00:14:28.801 align:start position:0%
<i> The team looking for Air France
 447 also used pinger locaters n
 an attempt to discover the crah
 site.</i>

00:14:34.205 --> 00:14:38.310 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> They begin listening
 for signals within days of
 the plane's disappearance.</i>

00:14:39.311 --> 00:14:45.050 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
OLIVIER: In most cases the
pinger locaters work, and that
was our assumption before

00:14:45.083 --> 00:14:47.052
Air France 447.

00:14:47.085 --> 00:14:54.159 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> NARRATOR: Ships scour
 the search area for forty
 days but hear nothing.</i>

00:14:54.192 --> 00:14:59.765 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> The hunters wonder:
 are the locater beacons
 lost, or destroyed?</i>

00:15:03.101 --> 00:15:08.874
<i> Returning to the remarkable
 drained seascape of the Air
 France 447 wreckage reveals</i>

00:15:08.907 --> 00:15:12.244
<i> the scale of the problem.</i>

00:15:12.277 --> 00:15:19.551 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> The sea floor here is 12,800
 feet below the surface</i>

00:15:19.584 --> 00:15:24.690
<i> deep in the mid-Atlantic ridge.</i>

00:15:24.723 --> 00:15:30.963
<i> And here amid the wreckage,
 nestling in the remains of the
 tail plane, the black boxes,</i>

00:15:30.996 --> 00:15:34.800
<i> one with its locater
 beacon still attached.</i>

00:15:34.833 --> 00:15:40.772
<i> A search ship passed directly
 over here, but detected nothin.</i>

00:15:40.805 --> 00:15:45.577 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> The metal wreckage may have
 blocked the signals, or they
 could have been damaged</i>

00:15:45.610 --> 00:15:48.280 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> by the sheer force of the
 impact.</i>

00:15:48.313 --> 00:15:53.118 align:start size:84% position:16%
OLIVIER: I think
they were unfortunately
damaged, at that stage.

00:15:57.956 --> 00:16:02.594
<i> NARRATOR: Investigators
 listening for the MH370
 black boxes can only hope</i>

00:16:02.627 --> 00:16:05.731
<i> they'll have better luck.</i>

00:16:05.764 --> 00:16:11.069 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> They monitor the ocean
 for fifteen days, using three
 ships equipped with sensitive</i>

00:16:11.102 --> 00:16:18.043
<i> listening technology,
 and military aircraft
 launching sonar buoys.</i>

00:16:18.076 --> 00:16:24.149 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> They do detect some
 signals, but conclude it's
 just random interference.</i>

00:16:26.184 --> 00:16:27.953
<i> It's another disappointment.</i>

00:16:30.522 --> 00:16:35.594 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> But at this low point
 there's a dramatic new lead.</i>

00:16:39.397 --> 00:16:45.003
<i> A team from Curtin University
 in Western Australia runs a
 network of sensitive underwater</i>

00:16:45.036 --> 00:16:55.013 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> microphones called
 hydrophones positioned all
 around the Australian coast.</i>

00:16:55.046 --> 00:17:02.654
<i> They're designed to monitor
 earthquakes, Antarctic ice and
 wildlife and, remarkably,</i>

00:17:02.687 --> 00:17:07.159
<i> one of them has picked
 up an unusual noise</i>

00:17:07.192 --> 00:17:16.134
<i> 1 hour and 14 minutes after
 MH370's last communication
 with the Inmarsat satellite.</i>

00:17:16.167 --> 00:17:22.074 line:0%
ALEC: When we looked at the
data from that hydrophone we
found one signal that looked

00:17:22.107 --> 00:17:26.511 align:start size:94% position:6%
as though it had interesting
characteristics.

00:17:26.544 --> 00:17:31.183
<i> NARRATOR: Is this the actual
 sound of MH370's final moments?</i>

00:17:39.324 --> 00:17:42.594 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Alec Duncan's team look for
 other audio recordings,</i>

00:17:42.627 --> 00:17:46.832 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> so they can calculate a
 geographical fix.</i>

00:17:46.865 --> 00:17:51.336 align:start size:97% position:3%
ALEC: One hydrophone does tell
you a certain amount about the
source of the signal,

00:17:51.369 --> 00:17:55.607 align:start size:94% position:6%
so you can tell whether it's
a whale, you can tell whether
it's a snapping shrimp,

00:17:55.640 --> 00:18:01.713 align:start size:94% position:6%
you can tell whether it's
just noise from the mooring,
but you can't tell direction.

00:18:03.515 --> 00:18:07.853
<i> NARRATOR: Fortunately, other
 agencies are listening too,</i>

00:18:07.886 --> 00:18:13.592
(explosion sound)

00:18:13.625 --> 00:18:17.362 align:start position:0%
<i> for the sound of illegal nucler
 tests</i>

00:18:19.430 --> 00:18:23.669
<i> with technology that can
 accurately pinpoint the source.</i>

00:18:26.671 --> 00:18:32.077 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> Just of the coast of Cape
 Leeuwin in Western Australia
 the Comprehensive Nuclear Test</i>

00:18:32.110 --> 00:18:40.352
<i> Ban Treaty Organisation runs
 a listening post specifically
 designed to detect and locate</i>

00:18:40.385 --> 00:18:42.488
<i> illegal nuclear tests.</i>

00:18:44.322 --> 00:18:52.297
<i> The station has three
 hydrophones set one and
 a quarter miles apart.</i>

00:18:52.330 --> 00:18:58.470
<i> They can determine the
 direction of a sound to
 within half a degree</i>

00:18:58.503 --> 00:19:03.208 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> and calculate the distance to
 the source, over thousands of
 miles.</i>

00:19:05.944 --> 00:19:13.085
<i> And astonishingly, this network
 has detected the exact noise
 heard by the team from Curtin
 University.</i>

00:19:17.155 --> 00:19:22.294 align:start size:97% position:3%
ALEC: The analysis of the data
from Cape Leeuwin told us that
that signal had come

00:19:22.327 --> 00:19:28.033 align:start position:0%
from the northwest and gave us a
fairly accurate bearing

00:19:28.066 --> 00:19:31.837 align:start size:94% position:6%
along which the source of the
signal must have been.

00:19:33.238 --> 00:19:35.607
<i> NARRATOR: It's a
 tantalizing clue</i>

00:19:35.640 --> 00:19:40.712
<i> and the sound does come from te
 direction of the seventh arc.</i>

00:19:40.745 --> 00:19:48.520
<i> But when the scientists crunch
 the numbers, the source of the
 sound is far to the northwest</i>

00:19:48.553 --> 00:19:54.326 align:start position:0%
<i> so far beyond the current searh
 area that it's considered not
 worth further investigation.</i>

00:19:55.393 --> 00:20:01.399 align:start size:94% position:6%
ALEC: Our conclusion was that
he signal was most likely
to be of geological origin,

00:20:01.432 --> 00:20:05.804 align:start size:88% position:13%
such as a small under sea
earthquake but we can't
completely rule out

00:20:05.837 --> 00:20:09.875
the possibility that it
came from something to
do with the aircraft.

00:20:15.980 --> 00:20:21.886
<i> NARRATOR: Faced with a new
 disappointment, the search team
 moves to a different approach:</i>

00:20:21.919 --> 00:20:28.627
<i> searching the sea floor
 itself, using towed sleds
 carrying sonar scanners.</i>

00:20:33.765 --> 00:20:37.636 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> It will be the largest
 undersea hunt in history.</i>

00:20:37.669 --> 00:20:43.241 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> But before it can even start
 there's a new problem.</i>

00:20:43.274 --> 00:20:48.947 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> This part of the Indian
 Ocean has never been
 surveyed in detail.</i>

00:20:48.980 --> 00:20:56.154 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> The sleds will be close to the
 sea floor and no one knows
 exactly what is down there.</i>

00:20:57.188 --> 00:21:00.392
PETER: We knew we'd be
operating vehicles very
close to the sea floor,

00:21:00.425 --> 00:21:04.229 align:start size:97% position:3%
which necessitated knowledge of
what was down there and

00:21:04.262 --> 00:21:08.233
so that we could avoid
collisions with terrain.

00:21:08.266 --> 00:21:15.841 align:start position:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The MH370 team need a
 simple way to create a snapshot
 of the sea floor across the vat
 search area.</i>

00:21:18.309 --> 00:21:21.113
<i> They find help in
 an unexpected place.</i>

00:21:27.719 --> 00:21:31.923 line:0%
<i> (music)</i>

00:21:31.956 --> 00:21:41.466
<i> The team hunting MH370 across
 463,000 square miles of Indian
 Ocean, face one big problem.</i>

00:21:41.499 --> 00:21:47.238 line:0%
WALTER: More than 99 percent
of the sea floor area in this
region has not been covered by

00:21:47.271 --> 00:21:52.277 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
ships with modern equipment,
satellite navigation,
multibeam echo sounding.

00:21:52.310 --> 00:21:57.449 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: Geophysicist Walter
 Smith thinks he might be
 able to fill in the blanks,</i>

00:21:57.482 --> 00:21:59.384
<i> using satellites.</i>

00:22:02.587 --> 00:22:06.958 align:start size:97% position:3%
WALTER: The satellites actually
cannot see the bottom, they're
not seeing the ocean floor,

00:22:06.991 --> 00:22:12.030
what they're doing is using
radar to measure variations
in the sea surface height.

00:22:12.063 --> 00:22:16.201 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> NARRATOR: It's possible,
 thanks to gravity.</i>

00:22:16.234 --> 00:22:21.306 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Mountains on the sea
 floor create their own
 gravitational attraction.</i>

00:22:21.339 --> 00:22:29.047 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Surrounding water is drawn
 towards the mass, creating
 measurable changes on the
 surface.</i>

00:22:29.080 --> 00:22:34.853
WALTER: The smallest ones I can
resolve are a kilometre or less
in height and they move the sea

00:22:34.886 --> 00:22:39.357 align:start size:91% position:9%
surface about 5 centimetres
and the satellite has
no problem resolving that.

00:22:39.390 --> 00:22:43.294
<i> NARRATOR: It doesn't have
 the resolution to spot the
 wreckage of an aircraft,</i>

00:22:43.327 --> 00:22:50.969 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> but it's enough to help create
 a basic map of the sea floor
 and identify hazards in the
 MH370 search zone.</i>

00:22:53.471 --> 00:22:56.841
<i> What does the data reveal?</i>

00:22:56.874 --> 00:23:03.248
<i> Using the exact information
 gathered by Walter Smith's team
 it's possible to drain away</i>

00:23:03.281 --> 00:23:08.720 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> the Indian Ocean to show what
 the ocean floor actually looks
 like.</i>

00:23:10.988 --> 00:23:16.428 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> As the water flows away it
 exposes a world no human
 has ever seen before.</i>

00:23:17.595 --> 00:23:24.436 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Dramatic cliffs, more
 than twice the height of
 the Empire State Building.</i>

00:23:24.469 --> 00:23:29.541 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Fault lines plunging almost
 20,000 feet deeper
 than any canyon on land.</i>

00:23:33.644 --> 00:23:39.484 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> A volcano-dotted valley
 running for a hundred miles.</i>

00:23:39.517 --> 00:23:45.056
<i> Operating underwater scanning
 vehicles in this terrain will
 take a huge amount of skill</i>

00:23:45.089 --> 00:23:46.291
<i> and luck.</i>

00:23:52.530 --> 00:23:59.571 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> Armed with their new map of
 the sea floor, the search team
 deploys the latest in subsea
 technology.</i>

00:23:59.604 --> 00:24:04.810 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Travelling deep underwater,
 these towed sleds begin
 to scan the sea floor.</i>

00:24:06.310 --> 00:24:10.348
<i> And they will be guided by
 a game-changing new clue.</i>

00:24:12.783 --> 00:24:20.525 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> It comes from further detailed
 analysis of the final Inmarsat
 signal from MH370.</i>

00:24:20.558 --> 00:24:25.396
<i> Engineers crunch the numbers,
 and discover something shockin.</i>

00:24:25.429 --> 00:24:31.069 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> In its final moments, the
 plane descends rapidly, almost
 certainly because after</i>

00:24:31.102 --> 00:24:34.906 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> seven and a half hours in the
 air, it's run out of fuel.</i>

00:24:37.108 --> 00:24:43.214 align:start position:0%
<i> Boeing engineers then simulate
 what happens when a triple sevn
 exhausts its fuel</i>

00:24:43.247 --> 00:24:52.290 align:start position:0%
<i> making it possible for the firt
 time to recreate the likely
 final minutes of MH370's
 mysterious flight.</i>

00:24:53.958 --> 00:24:58.796
<i> The right engine
 flames out first.</i>

00:24:58.829 --> 00:25:03.201 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> The autopilot compensates
 for the imbalance with a
 hard left turn.</i>

00:25:07.838 --> 00:25:11.209 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Minutes later the second
 engine flames out.</i>

00:25:13.611 --> 00:25:21.987 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> With no power, the autopilot
 switches off, leaving MH370
 in a long spiral decent.</i>

00:25:36.033 --> 00:25:39.070
(crashing sound)

00:25:50.348 --> 00:25:58.223
<i> In every Boeing simulation,
 the crash site lies within
 29 miles of the seventh arc.</i>

00:26:01.225 --> 00:26:06.497 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> These dramatic new insights
 immediately reduce the size
 of the search area,</i>

00:26:06.530 --> 00:26:13.438 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> from 463,000 square miles
 to just 23,000.</i>

00:26:13.471 --> 00:26:20.612
<i> But that's still three and a
 half times the size of the
 search area for Air France 447.</i>

00:26:20.645 --> 00:26:22.347 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
ANDY: This is a
massive search area.

00:26:22.380 --> 00:26:26.818 line:0%
It's magnitudes larger
than any previous search
I have ever worked on.

00:26:26.851 --> 00:26:30.989 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> NARRATOR: Deep sea salvage
 expert, Andy Sherrell analyses
 all the data</i>

00:26:31.022 --> 00:26:33.458 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> that's being gathered
 underwater.</i>

00:26:33.491 --> 00:26:38.196 align:start size:84% position:16%
ANDY: Oddly enough when
I first heard about
MH370's disappearance,

00:26:38.229 --> 00:26:41.933
my wife was in labour
with our first child.

00:26:41.966 --> 00:26:46.337 align:start size:91% position:9%
The news came on about the
disappearance of the flight
and my wife looked at me
and she said,

00:26:46.370 --> 00:26:49.774 align:start size:97% position:3%
'well that's a job you are not
going to be involved with.'

00:26:49.807 --> 00:26:53.845 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> NARRATOR: Andy has been
 involved ever since.</i>

00:26:53.878 --> 00:26:57.615 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Using his knowledge from six
 previous investigations,</i>

00:26:57.648 --> 00:27:01.886
<i> including a search for Amelia
 Earhart's Lockheed Electra.</i>

00:27:09.527 --> 00:27:15.733 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> As soon as the underwater
 scanning begins, it identifies
 shapes that could be manmade.</i>

00:27:15.766 --> 00:27:20.038 align:start size:97% position:3%
ANDY: So when you are reviewing
the side scan sonar it comes
down in a waterfall display

00:27:20.071 --> 00:27:26.411 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you can see the different
intensities of, you know
soft sand versus hard rock.

00:27:26.444 --> 00:27:33.284 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: Guided by the
 scans, the team investigate
 80 locations in detail</i>

00:27:33.317 --> 00:27:36.554
<i> but find no trace of MH370.</i>

00:27:38.389 --> 00:27:44.829 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Then in May 2015, the
 sonar picks up something
 new and exciting.</i>

00:27:44.862 --> 00:27:52.003
ANDY: It was a typical looking
debris field that definitely
warranted more investigation.

00:27:56.073 --> 00:28:05.817 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> NARRATOR: The debris field
 covers an area similar in size
 to the wreckage of Air France
 447.</i>

00:28:05.850 --> 00:28:09.921
<i> Could this finally be
 the remains of MH370?</i>

00:28:11.989 --> 00:28:14.759 align:start size:97% position:3%
PETER: Sonar paints a very good
picture of the sea floor,

00:28:14.792 --> 00:28:19.030 line:0%
and what you've got there but
it's not perfect. It's not good
enough to say with certainty

00:28:19.063 --> 00:28:23.167 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
that a certain item might
be an aircraft debris field.

00:28:23.200 --> 00:28:26.004 align:start size:97% position:3%
You actually have to
investigate with visual means.

00:28:29.340 --> 00:28:31.843 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> NARRATOR: They launch an
 autonomous underwater vehicle,</i>

00:28:31.876 --> 00:28:35.747
<i> armed with state of the art
 sensors, to take a closer look.</i>

00:28:38.649 --> 00:28:43.988 align:start position:0%
<i> Using the actual data it
 recorded that day, it's now
 possible to drain away the watr</i>

00:28:44.021 --> 00:28:51.863 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> from over the site, revealing
 the most hopeful discovery so
 far in the hunt for MH370.</i>

00:28:53.998 --> 00:28:57.668
<i> At one end a pile of chains.</i>

00:28:57.701 --> 00:29:02.106
<i> Then a 23 foot long metal box</i>

00:29:02.139 --> 00:29:07.645 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> rusted machinery and finally
 three metal anchors.</i>

00:29:09.113 --> 00:29:17.188
<i> They've found a shipwreck, its
 wooden hull long rotted away.</i>

00:29:17.221 --> 00:29:22.460
<i> The site strewn with
 coal, probably cargo,
 on its final voyage.</i>

00:29:23.994 --> 00:29:30.401 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> They haven't found MH370, but
 in these dark uncharted depths
 they're casting light on</i>

00:29:30.434 --> 00:29:32.771
<i> tragedies from a different age.</i>

00:29:37.541 --> 00:29:40.879 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> The investigators discover
 three other wrecks</i>

00:29:46.250 --> 00:29:52.223
<i> Including this iron
 hulled sailing ship
 upright on the seabed.</i>

00:29:57.561 --> 00:30:03.201 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> And a modern fishing
 trawler, nets stretched
 out across the sea floor</i>

00:30:04.101 --> 00:30:11.075
<i> proving that their technology
 can locate and retrieve objects
 as small as a piece of coal.</i>

00:30:15.045 --> 00:30:21.686 align:start position:0%
<i> After fifteen months of detaild
 scanning, covering 23,000
 square miles of sea floor,</i>

00:30:21.719 --> 00:30:24.823
<i> there's still no sign
 of the missing plane.</i>

00:30:26.357 --> 00:30:30.995 align:start size:94% position:6%
ANDY: We have a very high
confidence that we would have
detected the plane in that
search area.

00:30:31.028 --> 00:30:37.869 align:start position:0%
So all of these factors gives us
really high confidence that the
airplane debris field is not in
that area.

00:30:41.372 --> 00:30:47.211
<i> NARRATOR: The investigation
 continues, still guided by just
 two key pieces of evidence:</i>

00:30:47.244 --> 00:30:53.751 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> the Inmarsat calculations
 and Boeing's flight
 performance data.</i>

00:30:53.784 --> 00:30:58.223
<i> Then, suddenly, a third
 line of inquiry brings
 hope of a breakthrough.</i>

00:31:00.791 --> 00:31:05.396 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> On a remote island on the
 other side of the Indian
 Ocean, 3,000 miles</i>

00:31:05.429 --> 00:31:10.568 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> from the search area, a
 piece of metal washes up on
 a beach</i>

00:31:10.601 --> 00:31:13.438
<i> and it looks like it comes from
 an aircraft.</i>

00:31:16.674 --> 00:31:22.546 line:0%
<i> (music)</i>

00:31:22.579 --> 00:31:27.618 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> NARRATOR: In July
 2015, a council worker
 on Reunion Island,</i>

00:31:27.651 --> 00:31:34.792 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> a French territory in the
 Indian Ocean, stumbles upon
 an unusual object on a beach.</i>

00:31:34.825 --> 00:31:38.329
<i> It's called a flaperon.</i>

00:31:38.362 --> 00:31:48.606
<i> There is one on each wing of a
 Boeing triple seven part of the
 mechanism that makes it rise or
 fall.</i>

00:31:48.639 --> 00:31:55.813
<i> Boeing quickly confirms that
 its serial number matches
 that of the missing plane.</i>

00:31:55.846 --> 00:32:03.154 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> It's the first piece of
 physical evidence that MH370
 ended its flight in the ocean.</i>

00:32:03.187 --> 00:32:10.561 align:start size:97% position:3%
OFFICER: It is my hope that
this confirmation will at least
bring certainty to the families

00:32:10.594 --> 00:32:11.663
and loved ones.

00:32:15.165 --> 00:32:20.271
<i> NARRATOR: The suffering of the
 families attracts the attention
 of amateur wreck hunter</i>

00:32:20.304 --> 00:32:27.478
<i> Blaine Gibson and sets him off
 on a remarkable personal quest.</i>

00:32:27.511 --> 00:32:32.917 align:start position:0% line:0%
BLAINE: I was very, very
touched, very moved and realized
that I needed to go do

00:32:32.950 --> 00:32:38.789 align:start size:97% position:3%
what I could to solve this and
what I learned, what that niche
was,

00:32:38.822 --> 00:32:47.698 align:start size:97% position:3%
was that there was no official
search for debris that washed
ashore.

00:32:47.731 --> 00:32:54.638
<i> NARRATOR: Blaine consults
 ocean drift experts, who
 direct him to Mozambique</i>

00:32:54.671 --> 00:32:57.108
<i> where he makes an
 immediate discovery.</i>

00:32:59.843 --> 00:33:05.916
BLAINE: It was a triangular
grey shaped piece of debris
that said 'no step' on it.

00:33:05.949 --> 00:33:08.519
Clearly aircraft.

00:33:11.588 --> 00:33:20.831 align:start size:97% position:3%
I felt an enormous sense of
responsibility because I
realised that I had just found

00:33:20.864 --> 00:33:27.338 align:start size:91% position:9%
the second piece of debris
in the greatest aviation
mystery in history.

00:33:27.371 --> 00:33:32.810 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> NARRATOR: Boeing confirms
 that the "no step"
 debris is from MH370.</i>

00:33:32.843 --> 00:33:40.284
<i> Blaine has found it 4,350 miles
 away from the main search zone.</i>

00:33:45.089 --> 00:33:48.559
<i> Still acting alone, he
 continues to search.</i>

00:33:51.762 --> 00:33:56.834 align:start size:97% position:3%
BLAINE: We just found on Raike
Beach this piece of debris.

00:33:56.867 --> 00:33:58.603
We have not picked it up yet.

00:34:00.838 --> 00:34:06.977 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> NARRATOR: Almost thirty pieces
 of debris thought to come from
 MH370 are recovered.</i>

00:34:07.010 --> 00:34:11.115 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> All on the western side
 of the Indian Ocean.</i>

00:34:11.148 --> 00:34:14.585 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> Blaine Gibson finds
 fifteen of them.</i>

00:34:15.619 --> 00:34:18.589
<i> One piece stands
 out from the rest.</i>

00:34:18.622 --> 00:34:23.794 align:start size:91% position:9%
BLAINE: The most
significant to me, and
also to the investigation,

00:34:23.827 --> 00:34:30.734
was the case around the
TV screen on the back of
the seat in front of you.

00:34:30.767 --> 00:34:34.205 align:start size:88% position:13%
This is the one that
brought tears to my eyes.

00:34:34.238 --> 00:34:38.876
This is perhaps the last
thing that somebody saw.

00:34:38.909 --> 00:34:44.915
This is what anyone who flies
on a plane would recognize.

00:34:44.948 --> 00:34:48.719 align:start position:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Debris like this, frm
 inside the passenger cabin,</i>

00:34:48.752 --> 00:34:53.724 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> confirms that MH370 hit
 the water hard.</i>

00:34:53.757 --> 00:34:59.396 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
PETER: Some of the items
indicates that there was quite
a large amount of energy

00:34:59.429 --> 00:35:02.967 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
at the time the aircraft
entered the water.

00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:07.471 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> NARRATOR: Another
 piece of debris helps
 resolve a key question.</i>

00:35:07.504 --> 00:35:11.408 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Investigators already believe
 the plane ran out of fuel.</i>

00:35:11.441 --> 00:35:18.215 align:start position:0%
<i> But as it fell towards the
 ocean, was someone trying to
 save it with a controlled ditc?</i>

00:35:18.248 --> 00:35:24.488
PETER: This section of main
flap was found in Tanzania,
Pemba Island in 2016 and

00:35:24.521 --> 00:35:30.227 align:start size:91% position:9%
when imagery was passed to
us we realized it was
pretty significant.

00:35:30.260 --> 00:35:34.498 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: The flap extends on
 an internal support track.</i>

00:35:34.531 --> 00:35:40.571 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Investigators discover that
 the track has left impact
 marks inside the flap.</i>

00:35:40.604 --> 00:35:46.810
<i> That can only happen if it is
 in a retracted position strong
 evidence that the plane was</i>

00:35:46.843 --> 00:35:51.048
<i> in its normal cruising state
 as it fell towards the sea.</i>

00:35:54.918 --> 00:35:57.054 align:start size:91% position:9%
PETER: It's been the
subject of a lot of debate.

00:35:57.087 --> 00:36:02.226
The hard physical evidence
and our analysis showed that
it wasn't a controlled ditch,

00:36:02.259 --> 00:36:06.363 align:start size:97% position:3%
or there wasn't active control
from the cockpit extending the
flaps at the time

00:36:06.396 --> 00:36:09.367 align:start size:94% position:6%
the flaps separated from the
aircraft.

00:36:12.269 --> 00:36:17.174
<i> NARRATOR: The flap supports
 the theory that the plane ran
 out of fuel and suggests that</i>

00:36:17.207 --> 00:36:21.212
<i> no one at that point was trying
 to ditch or save it.</i>

00:36:27.651 --> 00:36:33.757
<i> But the debris doesn't only
 confirm how MH370 crashed.</i>

00:36:33.790 --> 00:36:40.264 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> It opens up a new and
 exciting line of enquiry that
 could narrow the search area.</i>

00:36:44.835 --> 00:36:48.372
DAVID: I think it's enormously
important to find the plane,

00:36:48.405 --> 00:36:52.776 align:start size:91% position:9%
because while planes crash
routinely,

00:36:52.809 --> 00:36:58.015 line:0%
they don't just disappear and
I think this terrifies people.

00:36:58.048 --> 00:37:03.153
<i> NARRATOR: Oceanographer David
 Griffin believes that the very
 first piece of debris recovere,</i>

00:37:03.186 --> 00:37:07.891
<i> the flaperon on Reunion
 Island, may be the key.</i>

00:37:07.924 --> 00:37:09.293
SKIPPER: This'd be a
good spot, I reckon.

00:37:09.326 --> 00:37:12.563 align:start size:91% position:9%
The wind's probably a
bit stronger, I guess. Yep.

00:37:12.596 --> 00:37:18.168
DAVID: This is a Boeing
777 flaperon off another
aircraft, not MH370.

00:37:18.201 --> 00:37:21.472
SKIPPER: We use this anchor
to sink it again, alright?

00:37:21.505 --> 00:37:26.410 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> NARRATOR: David Griffin is
 trying to recreate the journey
 of the flaperon across the
 ocean,</i>

00:37:26.443 --> 00:37:30.514
<i> in the hope this will
 pinpoint the crash site.</i>

00:37:35.052 --> 00:37:39.123
<i> He compares the flaperon's
 movement to these buoys.</i>

00:37:39.156 --> 00:37:46.830 align:start position:0%
<i> Known as ocean drifters, they'e
 used daily all around the world
 to monitor ocean currents.</i>

00:37:46.863 --> 00:37:50.367 align:start size:94% position:6%
DAVID: We very quickly
determined that these replica
flaperons moved about

00:37:50.400 --> 00:37:56.607
10 centimetres per second
faster downwind than an
oceanographic drifter.

00:37:57.908 --> 00:38:02.112
<i> NARRATOR: That may not sound
 like much, but when he compares
 it to known data about</i>

00:38:02.145 --> 00:38:07.217 align:start position:0%
<i> the currents in the seventh arc
 on the day of the crash it hels
 him estimate the flaperon's</i>

00:38:07.250 --> 00:38:10.988
<i> travels during the 500
 days it was afloat.</i>

00:38:17.527 --> 00:38:23.000
<i> It's an extraordinary discover.</i>

00:38:23.033 --> 00:38:26.937 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> To narrow the search zone
 even, further David Griffin
 investigates</i>

00:38:26.970 --> 00:38:30.674 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> another strange feature of the
 debris.</i>

00:38:30.707 --> 00:38:36.080
DAVID: In addition to
knowing where things
are found in Africa,

00:38:36.113 --> 00:38:41.452 align:start size:91% position:9%
the other key thing is
where things have not been
found and that's Australia.

00:38:41.485 --> 00:38:46.523 align:start position:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Why has no debris
 been washed ashore in Australi?</i>

00:38:46.556 --> 00:38:51.595
<i> Some of the ocean currents
 in the search area flow
 east towards the country.</i>

00:38:51.628 --> 00:38:57.134
<i> A plane crashing here
 should leave evidence
 on Australian beaches.</i>

00:38:57.167 --> 00:39:01.839 align:start size:91% position:9%
DAVID: So this immediately
tells us that all those
potential parts

00:39:01.872 --> 00:39:06.210 align:start size:94% position:6%
of the seventh arc, where the
flow is strongly towards
Australia,

00:39:06.243 --> 00:39:07.745
they're essentially ruled out.

00:39:11.681 --> 00:39:16.387 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: When David Griffin
 looks at the historic drifter
 data from the day MH370</i>

00:39:16.420 --> 00:39:21.525
<i> disappeared he finds three
 places on the seventh arc where
 the current would have pushed</i>

00:39:21.558 --> 00:39:26.163 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> debris away from
 Australia towards Africa.</i>

00:39:26.196 --> 00:39:32.269 align:start size:97% position:3%
DAVID: Here around 35, you have
this area where everything is
moving to the west.

00:39:32.302 --> 00:39:36.206 align:start size:94% position:6%
Again up at 31, everything's
moving to the west.

00:39:36.239 --> 00:39:41.678
<i> NARRATOR: If the plane crashed
 in one of these areas, it would
 explain why debris was not foud</i>

00:39:41.711 --> 00:39:44.281
<i> in Australia.</i>

00:39:44.314 --> 00:39:49.920 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> And critically, only one of
 these locations matches his
 calculation of the flaperon's
 journey.</i>

00:39:51.755 --> 00:39:56.427 align:start size:97% position:3%
DAVID: So that led us to
propose that 35 south is the
most likely area for the crash.

00:40:00.363 --> 00:40:08.472
<i> NARRATOR: 35 south is an area
 that was not checked during the
 initial surface debris search.</i>

00:40:08.505 --> 00:40:12.376
<i> For investigators, it's
 an exciting new lead.</i>

00:40:12.409 --> 00:40:14.578
<i> But it comes too late.</i>

00:40:14.611 --> 00:40:18.515 align:start size:97% position:3%
MINISTER: It is with some level
of sadness, certainly with a
great deal of frustration

00:40:18.548 --> 00:40:21.952 align:start size:97% position:3%
and disappointment that I stand
here and acknowledge that the
search,

00:40:21.985 --> 00:40:25.556
the underwater search area
effort, has been suspended.

00:40:25.589 --> 00:40:32.596
<i> NARRATOR: On January the
 17th 2017, after almost
 three frustrating years,</i>

00:40:32.629 --> 00:40:36.333
<i> the search for MH
 370 is called off.</i>

00:40:36.366 --> 00:40:42.039
ANDY: Obviously I had strong
feelings and am disappointed
that we didn't find he aircraft

00:40:42.072 --> 00:40:48.245
but it's not for the technical
abilities or the giving it full
effort and feeling good about

00:40:48.278 --> 00:40:50.747
the job that we did at the time.

00:40:50.780 --> 00:40:55.719
PETER: It was record breaking
in terms of the time it took
and the area that was covered.

00:40:55.752 --> 00:41:00.157
There's never been a search as
big by an order of magnitude.

00:41:04.528 --> 00:41:09.766 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: But will David
 Griffin's flaperon experiment
 persuade authorities</i>

00:41:09.799 --> 00:41:12.269
<i> to search 35 degrees south?</i>

00:41:14.638 --> 00:41:21.312 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> It takes almost a year
 to answer that question, and
 new evidence from the skies.</i>

00:41:25.749 --> 00:41:33.657 line:0%
<i> (music)</i>

00:41:33.690 --> 00:41:38.595 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Oceanographer David
 Griffin has a new theory.</i>

00:41:38.628 --> 00:41:44.868
<i> He thinks MH370 is in
 an area called 35 south.</i>

00:41:44.901 --> 00:41:50.107 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> And in 2017, he finds
 more evidence pointing
 to the same place.</i>

00:41:51.308 --> 00:41:57.214
DAVID: I remember the day when
I first saw those images in high
resolution and I thought my God,

00:41:57.247 --> 00:41:59.483
this, this really helps.

00:42:01.952 --> 00:42:07.558
<i> NARRATOR: Fifteen days after
 MH 370 disappeared, a French
 military satellite captured</i>

00:42:07.591 --> 00:42:16.333 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> images of possible
 manmade debris in the
 vicinity of 35 south.</i>

00:42:16.366 --> 00:42:23.473 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> At that time, the location
 wasn't considered a high
 priority area.</i>

00:42:23.506 --> 00:42:28.278
<i> But, four years later,
 it's a different story.</i>

00:42:28.311 --> 00:42:30.647
DAVID: And I thought that
this is really exciting.

00:42:30.680 --> 00:42:33.584
This could be used to
restart the search.

00:42:38.321 --> 00:42:41.091 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> NARRATOR: A new year
 brings new hope.</i>

00:42:41.124 --> 00:42:48.933 align:start position:0%
<i> In January 2018, salvage compay
 Ocean Infinity sets out on a
 brand new, privately funded,
 mission.</i>

00:42:51.801 --> 00:42:54.972
<i> The seventh arc is
 still the focus.</i>

00:42:55.005 --> 00:43:00.644 align:start position:0%
<i> But they expand the width of te
 previous search zone and move
 towards the new area</i>

00:43:00.677 --> 00:43:04.314
<i> identified by David Griffin.</i>

00:43:04.347 --> 00:43:13.691 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Once again, Andy Sherrell is
 on board and this time he has
 eight autonomous underwater
 vehicles or AUVs.</i>

00:43:15.425 --> 00:43:21.198 align:start position:0%
<i> ANDY: One deep towed vessel
 would do a swing of about thiry
 days on site and over those</i>

00:43:21.231 --> 00:43:26.036
<i> thirty days on sight they would
 cover about 35 hundred square
 kilometres.</i>

00:43:26.069 --> 00:43:31.542
<i> Using eight AUVs simultaneously
 we can cover that in about
 three to four days.</i>

00:43:33.677 --> 00:43:35.712 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> NARRATOR: It's a
 high stakes gamble.</i>

00:43:35.745 --> 00:43:41.952
<i> If they find nothing,
 they don't get paid.</i>

00:43:41.985 --> 00:43:47.324
<i> Ocean Infinity searches the
 new priority area at 35 south.</i>

00:43:51.761 --> 00:43:59.269
<i> In five months they cover an
 area almost equal in size to
 the original sea floor search.</i>

00:44:02.238 --> 00:44:07.711
<i> But frustratingly, they
 find no trace of MH370.</i>

00:44:11.981 --> 00:44:18.589 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> The two search efforts
 have covered almost 92,000
 square miles of sea floor</i>

00:44:19.589 --> 00:44:25.362
<i> fourteen times the size of the
 Air France 447 search zone.</i>

00:44:25.395 --> 00:44:32.602
<i> (music)</i>

00:44:32.635 --> 00:44:40.610 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> In May 2018 the Malaysian
 government announces that
 it will not support any new
 searches.</i>

00:44:40.643 --> 00:44:47.617 align:start size:97% position:3%
OFFICIAL: We cannot keep on
searching for this 370 forever.

00:44:47.650 --> 00:44:52.055
<i> NARRATOR: Is the wreckage of
 MH370 waiting to be discovered</i>

00:44:52.088 --> 00:45:01.498 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> in the 386, 000 square miles
 of seventh arc search zone
 that remains unexplored?</i>

00:45:01.531 --> 00:45:07.838
<i> Do clues to the mystery lie a
 few feet outside the circle of
 light and knowledge already cat</i>

00:45:07.871 --> 00:45:10.774
<i> by the search teams?</i>

00:45:10.807 --> 00:45:20.817 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Only time, money and the
 continued desire for answers
 separate us from the moment
 of discovery.</i>

00:45:20.850 --> 00:45:25.922 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> But the knowledge, hard
 won over two search
 efforts and four years,</i>

00:45:25.955 --> 00:45:29.326
<i> will be invaluable
 for any future hunt.</i>

00:45:31.928 --> 00:45:36.533 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Using the extraordinary
 data gathered by hundreds
 of investigators,</i>

00:45:36.566 --> 00:45:43.774 align:start position:0%
<i> we can drain the area around te
 seventh arc to reveal what MH30
 might look like</i>

00:45:43.807 --> 00:45:48.178
<i> when it is found.</i>

00:45:48.211 --> 00:45:53.216 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> The debris will stretch out
 over thousands of yards.</i>

00:45:53.249 --> 00:45:57.287 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> The engines will
 be in one piece, the
 fuselage in thousands.</i>

00:46:00.957 --> 00:46:06.029 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> The nearby black boxes
 will reveal if anyone was
 in the cockpit at the end.</i>

00:46:09.132 --> 00:46:14.371 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Emergency oxygen cylinders
 may show if the plane
 depressurized in flight,</i>

00:46:14.404 --> 00:46:17.741
<i> whether intentionally or
 the result of an accident.</i>

00:46:19.342 --> 00:46:26.683 align:start position:0%
<i> And personal electronic devices
 could reveal the experiences ad
 fate of individual passengers
 and crew.</i>

00:46:29.219 --> 00:46:35.692 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> It will be a profound moment,
 one that Andy Sherrell has
 experienced before.</i>

00:46:35.725 --> 00:46:39.729 line:0%
ANDY: It's a really surreal,
kind of sombre moment because
you realise all of a sudden

00:46:39.762 --> 00:46:46.469 line:0%
that you have found this you,
gravesite, this place where
all these people perished.

00:46:46.502 --> 00:46:51.241 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
It's sad but it's also a bit of
relief because you know that
from that point forward

00:46:51.274 --> 00:46:53.844 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
some answers are going to come
back.

00:46:53.877 --> 00:46:57.314 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
OLIVIER: I think you have to
be to remain optimistic,
and if you don't search,

00:46:57.347 --> 00:46:58.548 line:0%
you don't find.

00:46:58.581 --> 00:47:01.285 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
So hopefully at the end
you will find it.

